  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather continues into the middle of this week \nwith a gradual warming trend. \n\nA well-defined area of high pressure is still just off the NJ coast \nearly this morning, with sfc ridging extending SSW into the\nlocal area. As a result, there is a very weak pressure gradient\nand light/calm winds at this hour. A weak shortwave aloft over \nthe central Appalachians continues to slowly move toward the \nlocal area. High clouds have overspread much of VA/NC in advance\nof this feature. Temperatures are in the upper 30s-40s and will\nnot fall much more overnight due to the cloud cover. Radar \nmosaic shows some light echoes over SE WV and SW VA, with some \nsites in SW VA reporting light rain at this hour. Clouds \nincrease today as that shortwave weakens as it approaches and \ncrosses the area. Highs will be only in the mid to upper 60s due\nto the clouds. There is a very low but nonzero chance of light \nrain in the Piedmont later this morning- midday but rain amounts\nwould only be a few hundredths of an inch at best. Temps may \nstruggle to get out of the lower 60s across western portions of \nthe FA if we manage to see some very light rain. \n\nA pronounced warming trend takes place on Tuesday/Wednesday as a \nbuilding ridge aloft moves over the area while the surface high \nmoves farther offshore. With the low-level flow becoming SE, lower \nto mid 70s are expected on Tuesday with mid to upper 70s on \nWednesday.  Depending on cloud cover, 80F readings are across \nportions of interior srn VA and NE NC Wednesday. Overnight lows also \ngradually moderate from the 40s tonight to around 60F Wednesday \nnight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chance for showers returns Thursday and Friday, \nbut confidence in widespread beneficial rainfall remains low, though \nmost areas likely see at least 0.10\" of rain. Above average \ntemperatures are also expected to continue through at least the \nend of the week.\n\nA strong low pressure system at the surface and aloft will initially \nmove from the Rockies into the northern Plains and Midwest early in \nthe week, while a trailing cold front eventually tries to advance \ntoward the area in the Thursday-Friday timeframe. Shower chances \nreturn on Thursday as the trailing cold front gets close to the \narea. Showers could begin by late morning west of I-95, with \nprecipitation chances expanding to cover the entire area by the end \nof the day. However, most of the models show a weakening trend in \nthe showers as they approach the area as the best upper forcing \nmisses us to the north. Despite this, most areas will likely see at \nleast 0.10\" of rain from Thursday-Thursday night. Shower chances \nlikely end across the Piedmont by Friday morning following the FROPA \nbut continue during the day/evening on Friday across the eastern 2/3 \nof the FA (especially near the coast) as some redevelopment is \npossible before the weak cold front crosses the rest of the area. \nAgain, expect more in the way of scattered showers versus widespread \nrain from Thu-Fri. Most areas will see measurable rain but ensemble \nprobabilities for >0.5\" are only 10-30% on the EPS and 30-60% on the \nGEFS/CMCE. Highs remain at least in the low-mid 70s through the end \nof the week, with dew points potentially rising well into the 60s by \nlate week. Mainly dry weather likely returns on Saturday. Another \nshortwave may move across the area late Sunday-Monday, but this \nsystem will likely be moisture starved as the deeper moisture likely \ngets shunted offshore after Friday's FROPA.\n\nThere won't be much in the way of appreciable CAA behind the front, \nand above average temperatures may continue through next weekend. \nThere remain no potential freezes in the forecast as the more \nsubstantial cold air remains in the northern half of Canada for the \nforeseeable future.",
